        "# EXERCISE 1 — Generative vs Discriminative Models\n",
        "\n",
        "You are helping a team of biologists in Antarctica. They measure penguins in the field and want to know **which species each penguin is**: **Adelie** or **Chinstrap**.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal:** Predict the species of a penguin using its body measurements.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a **supervised learning** problem: we have features (`BillLength`, `BillDepth`, `FlipperLength`, `BodyMass`, `Sex`) and a label (`Species`). You will train two kinds of classifiers and **visualize** how each one thinks:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Part 1 — Generative (Naive Bayes):** learns the **joint probability** of the features and the label. It learns the *shape* of each group (Adelie / Chinstrap).\n",
        "- **Part 2 — Discriminative (Logistic Regression):** learns the **conditional probability** of the label given the features **directly**. It learns a *boundary* that separates the groups.\n",
        "\n",
        "*Tip: the Demo notebook (`Demo_Generative&Discriminative.ipynb`) follows the same steps with a different dataset.*\n",
        "\n",
        "Dataset: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/parulpandey/palmer-archipelago-antarctica-penguin-data\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Objectives\n",
        "\n",
        "By the end of this exercise, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Explain the difference between a **generative** model (learns the joint probability) and a **discriminative** model (learns the conditional probability directly).\n",
        "2. Train a **Naive Bayes** model (generative) and a **Logistic Regression** model (discriminative) using scikit-learn.\n",
        "3. Make predictions on test data and measure **accuracy**.\n",
        "4. **Visualize** what each model learned: the *shape of each group* for the generative model, and the *decision boundary* for the discriminative model.\n",
        "5. Compare the two models.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Expected Outcomes\n",
        "\n",
        "When you finish, your notebook should have:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Section | Expected outcome |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| 3 & 6 | Two trained models: `nb` (Naive Bayes) and `logreg` (Logistic Regression) |\n",
        "| 4 & 7 | Test accuracy printed for each model: about **0.95** for Naive Bayes and about **1.00** for Logistic Regression |\n",
        "| 5 | A plot with **two separate filled shapes**: a blue one around the Adelie penguins (shorter bills) and a red one around the Chinstrap penguins (longer bills) |\n",
        "| 8 | A plot with the same dots and **one gray line** (the decision boundary) between the two species |\n",
        "| 9 | A comparison table and bar chart of both models |"
      ]
    },
